**Check the premises rather than confirming them.** Five of my "established" facts have been refuted
by readers in the last two days — one of them a premise I had already put to the owner, and one a
filename I derived from a row's id instead of reading the row's own title. **Where a premise names a
line, check it there and tell me if it is about the wrong thing.**

## THE EDIT

`dev_wdc.c`, the auxiliary-region registration — a literal `2` becomes `1`:

```c
	memory_device_register(devinit->machine->memory, "wdc_altstatus",
	    alt_status_addr, 1, dev_wdc_altstatus_access, d, DM_DEFAULT, NULL);
```

Offset 0 (Alternate Status read / Device Control write) is unchanged. **Offset 1 — the ATA-2 Drive
Address register — is never claimed**, so it falls to the unmapped path.

## WHY — and this is the part to attack, not the edit

**ATA-2 Note 1** (`ata2_x3t10_948d.txt:1529-1531`), reached via the Control Block map at `:1483-1495`
by ADDRESS rather than by name (a grep for "Drive Address" returns zero):

> *"This register is obsolete. It is recommended that a device not respond to a read of this address.
> If a device does respond, it shall be sure not to drive the DD7 signal to prevent possible conflict
> with floppy disk implementations."*

Today the model **responds**, aliasing the status byte, because `dev_wdc_altstatus_access` computes
`odata = status_byte(...)` before it looks at the offset and the common tail assigns it on any read.

## SETTLED — verified today; cite rather than re-derive, but say if any is wrong

* **The unmapped path does NOT fault.** `memory_rw.c:676-712`: an unmapped read `memset`s to zero,
  an unmapped write falls to `do_return_ok` ignored, and the MIPS `EXCEPTION_DBE` block at
  `:698-704` is inside `#if 0`. *(I previously told the owner this narrowing gives a bus fault. That
  was wrong — I had carried the registered-device `res <= 0` path's behaviour onto the unmapped
  path, which does not share it.)*
* **A wide access is SAFE and is reachable.** The dispatcher accepts it whole against the shorter
  region and clamps `len`; the `#95` pre-zero runs first with the caller's full `len`, so the
  guest-visible buffer is byte-for-byte `{status, 0…}` before and after. And it is genuinely
  reachable: `dev_ps2_spd.c:73-77` relays the guest's **own `len`** to `d->wdcaddr + 0x206`.
* **The edit is a literal `2` -> `1`, NOT `1 * addr_mult`** — the alt callback never divides its
  offset by `addr_mult`, and all five machine cases change the ADDRESS only.
* **It retires the open `wdcaltwidth` defect by elimination**: with a one-byte region the clamp
  forces `len == 1`, so that row's named escaping mutant (`data[0]` -> `data[len - 1]`) becomes
  EQUIVALENT. The warrant to record is *the clamp at `memory_rw.c:523`*.

## QUESTIONS

1. **Is "not respond" honestly modelled by an unmapped address that reads as zero?** The standard
   says *recommended not to respond*; an unmapped read here returns 0 rather than floating. **Is
   returning zero a response?** If it is, is there a better available disposition — and note
   `return 0` from a REGISTERED device is not available, because that is the fault path, which is a
   different thing entirely.
2. **DD7.** I claimed the narrowing satisfies the DD7 constraint. A reader has since told me
   *driving DD7 low is still driving DD7*, so bit-7-clear is not the electrical equivalent of
   leaving it undriven — and that after narrowing, an unconditional assertion about the guest's
   value at `A+1` is simply wrong, since another responder could legitimately set it. **Is the
   replacement oracle — ZERO WDC CONTRIBUTION, PROVEN BY ROUTING — constructible here, and what does
   it observe?**
3. **THE WITNESS INVERTS AND I WANT THAT CHECKED.** The existing rung-3 witness passes by showing
   INVARIANCE across head select (51 at head=0 and 51 at head=15) with an SDH readback of `0xaf`
   proving the head write landed. After narrowing, what does that witness show, and **is invariance
   still the right oracle or does it become uninformative?**
4. **What detector row ships with this fix?** The de-escalation clause says a fix that removes a
   host-reachable behaviour owes accept-side rows proving legal accesses still land. Offset 0 must
   still work in both directions. **Name the rows, and say which mutant each kills and what
   observable differs under it** — a row whose channel cannot vary under its own mutant is vacuous
   by construction, and that is knowable now, before anything runs.
5. **Is any MACHINE harmed?** Five machines set `alt_status_addr` differently. **Does any of them
   place something else at `A+1` that the two-byte registration was shadowing** — in which case
   narrowing changes which device answers, not merely whether one does?
6. **What should the round NOT claim?** The ATA-2 draft is **Revision 4c, unapproved**, and Note 1
   is a RECOMMENDATION rather than a requirement. Say what that licenses and what it does not.

