    1: """wdcaltoffset -- RUNG-3 WITNESS AND DETECTOR.  Real guest instructions, real address
    2: decode, real memory_rw, on an UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description (evbmips -e malta).
    3: 
    4: THE DEFECT.  DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus) in src/devices/dev_wdc.c reads relative_addr
    5: ZERO times, while dev_wdc_init registers its region TWO BYTES WIDE.  The durable keys are
    6: grep anchors, not line numbers:
    7: 
    8:     grep -n 'DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus)'   src/devices/dev_wdc.c
    9:     grep -n '"wdc_altstatus",'               src/devices/dev_wdc.c
   10: 
   11:         memory_device_register(..., "wdc_altstatus", alt_status_addr, 2,
   12:             dev_wdc_altstatus_access, d, DM_DEFAULT, NULL);
   13: 
   14:     offset 0  ->  0x3f6  Device Control      (write: nIEN, SRST, 4BIT)
   15:     offset 1  ->  0x3f7  Drive Address Reg   READ-ONLY on real ATA hardware
   16: 
   17: Both offsets land in the same handler body, so a guest STORE to 0x3f7 is executed as a
   18: store to Device Control.  Since #481 corrected the arm's bit to WDCTL_RST and wired in
   19: wdc_new_command_reset(), that store now performs a full soft reset: write_in_progress is
   20: cleared and the input ring is purged.  A write to a register the guest is not even
   21: allowed to write aborts a live ATA WRITE SECTORS and destroys its staged bytes.
   22: 
   23: THE MACHINE IS NOT MODIFIED AND NO DEVICE IS ADDED.  malta carries a wdc through its own
   24: committed path: machine_evbmips.c bus_pci_add(..., "piix4_ide") -> bus_pci.c
   25: PCIINIT(piix4_ide), which does device_add(machine, "wdc addr=<isa_portbase+0x1f0> ...")
   26: whenever an IDE image is attached; isa_portbase = 0x18000000.  Attaching a disk image is
   27: ordinary operation, not a machine edit.
   28: 
   29:     wdc registers      physical 0x180001f0 + N  ->  KSEG1 0xffffffffb80001f0 + N
   30:     wdc_altstatus      physical 0x180003f6      ->  KSEG1 0xffffffffb80003f6
   31:     the read-only reg  physical 0x180003f7      ->  KSEG1 0xffffffffb80003f7
   32: 
   33: THE SHAPE OF THE WITNESS -- FOUR phases, IDENTICAL except for the address of one
   34: store (phases 2 and 4 differ only in the VALUE stored).
   35: 
   36:     PHASE 1  poke 0x06 (nIEN|SRST) at 0x3f6, the REAL Device Control register.
   37:              A legitimate soft reset.  BOTH builds must abort the armed write.
   38:              This is the ACCEPT control the de-escalation clause demands: it proves the
   39:              fix leg does not disable the register it is supposed to leave alone, and it
   40:              proves the reset machinery this probe observes is alive.
   41: 
   42:     PHASE 2  poke the SAME 0x06 at 0x3f7, the READ-ONLY Drive Address Register.
   43:              *** THE DEFECT.  Committed build: identical to phase 1.  Fix leg: nothing
   44:              happens. ***
   45: 
   46:     PHASE 3  poke 0x02 (nIEN alone, WDCTL_RST clear) at 0x3f7.
   47:              Neither build resets.  This is the discriminator that says the defect is
   48:              the RESET BIT reaching a register that has none, rather than "any store to
   49:              0x3f7 does something".
   50: 
   51: PHASE 1 AND PHASE 2 ARE THE ANCHOR (vacuity-floor clause (f)).  They are the same
   52: stimulus at ADJACENT addresses, so the pair pins WHERE the store lands, not merely that
   53: two addresses behave differently.  On the committed build the two phases are
   54: indistinguishable -- which IS the defect, stated as a measurement.
   55: 
   56: THE TWO CONTROLS THE WITNESS LADDER DEMANDS FOR AN ACCESS WITNESS:
   57: 
   58:     LIVENESS          three of them.
   59:                       (a) RAM: the same sb/lbu instruction shapes round-trip 0x55
   60:                           through plain RAM, so a device row reading 0x00 could not be
   61:                           blamed on a bad encoding.
   62:                       (b) DEVICE REGISTER: 0x55 -> cyl_lo and 0x66 -> cyl_hi read back
   63:                           as 0x6655 through the wdc's own decode.  This is #481's
   64:                           preserved liveness=0x6655 idiom, kept deliberately so this
   65:                           witness and that one are comparable.
   66:                       (c) per phase: the ARMED status read, which must show DRQ before
   67:                           the poke or the phase measured nothing.
   68: 
   69:     DEVICE SIGNATURE  two of them.
   70:                       (a) FIFO: push 0x5a then 0xa5 to the data port and read the SAME
   71:                           address twice.  A memory cell returns 0xa5 both times; only a
   72:                           FIFO returns them in push order.
   73:                       (b) STATUS: the byte at +7 is COMPUTED by status_byte() out of
   74:                           diskimage_exist(), d->error and d->write_in_progress.  The
   75:                           guest never wrote it.  RAM would answer with what was stored.
   76: 
   77: WHY THIS IS ALSO A DETECTOR, NOT ONLY A WITNESS.  Every row carries a want for BOTH
   78: legs.  Phase 2's two rows are the only ones whose want DIFFERS between the legs; every
   79: other row is an identity row that must hold on both.  So the file fails on the committed
   80: build, passes once the handler decodes its offset, and still fails if a future fix
   81: over-reaches: a build that refuses offset 0 as well reds PHASE 1, and a build that keeps
   82: the decode but nulls its effect reds PHASE 2 again.
   83: 
   84: BYTE ORDER.  evbmips -e malta is LITTLE-endian.  Every access here is a single byte, so
   85: byte order cannot silently change what a row means -- stated because a sibling detector
   86: in this tree was measured testing the wrong endianness.
   87: 
   88: Usage:  wdcaltoff_rung3_probe.py <base-binary> <fix-binary> <disk-image> [outdir]
   89: 
   90: BOTH LEGS RUN INSIDE ONE PYTHON INVOCATION, SO THE INSTRUMENT IS IDENTICAL BY
   91: CONSTRUCTION.  A driver shaped "for t in base fix; do python3 probe.py $t; done" gives
   92: the two legs different instruments if the probe is edited between them, and the
   93: difference lands in the very output being diffed.
   94: 
   95: NOTHING IN THE REPO IS WRITTEN.  Both trees under measurement are copies under /tmp, and
   96: the disk image is attached with the iR: prefix, whose overlay is pid-unique.
   97: CONTRACT -- vacuity-floor clause (e).  What this detector is DESIGNED to catch, and
   98: what it is blind to.  Stated so a later escape is either outside this contract or a
   99: breach of it, rather than an argument about whether the round overclaimed.
  100: 
  101:   CATCHES: edits to the offset decode of the wdc_altstatus WRITE arm.
  102:     * widening the decline so it also refuses offset 0   -> P1 accept rows red
  103:     * nulling or narrowing it so a store addressed at offset 1 reaches the reset
  104:       again -> P2 and P4 red, via DRQ and the input ring
  105:     * value-conditioned guards ("decline only the value we have seen") -> P4 exists
  106:       for exactly this: its store carries the bare reset bit 0x04, not the 0x06 the
  107:       witness and #481's comment both use
  108: 
  109:   BLIND BY CONSTRUCTION -- each of these is another row's job or another row's absence:
  110:     * PARTIAL-GUARD edits that clobber d->cur_command but spare the ring.  NO row here
  111:       observes cur_command.  That is wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py's contract, and it
  112:       needs a ciR: CD-ROM rig.  Measured: such a mutant passes all 18 rows here.
  113:     * READS of the alt region.  No row reads either offset.  Tracked as wdcaltdigin.
  114:     * MULTI-BYTE stores.  Every row is len == 1; a len == 2 store at offset 0 passes
  115:       the decode and its second byte is consumed silently.  Tracked as wdcaltwidth.
  116:     * the DIAGNOSTIC TEXT.  No row reads debug output, so an edit that declines
